\subsection{Scale-Dependent Convergence}\label{ssec:scale_convergence}

We compare the LAMBADA convergence pattern at two scales. At 1.3B parameters, convergence is monotonic: $\sigma$ decreases at every attention level from 0\% through 25\% ($\rho = -1.0$). At 340M, convergence is partial. $\sigma$ reaches a maximum reduction of 56\% at intermediate attention ratios, but rebounds at h3-1, yielding a net endpoint reduction of 36\%: the trajectory is non-monotonic, and the highest-attention model does not achieve the lowest cross-architecture variance. One possible interpretation is that at smaller scale, the capacity overhead of attention layers creates interference that offsets the retrieval benefit.

This contrast indicates that sufficient scale is a necessary condition for complete equalization on LAMBADA. We emphasize that this is a binary observation from two data points, not a scaling law. Characterizing the functional relationship between scale and convergence requires intermediate scales beyond the scope of this study. Despite the aggregate divergence, within-scale pairwise Jaccard similarity between architectures' per-example effects is nearly identical at both scales (helped: 0.094 at 340M vs.\ 0.096 at 1.3B; hurt: 0.090 at both). Per-example architecture specificity is stable across scales even when aggregate convergence trajectories differ, extending the decoupling observation from the attention-ratio dimension (\S\ref{ssec:decoupling}) to the scale dimension. Full cross-scale comparisons appear in Appendix~E.

